Abstract
Atom transfer radical polymerization (ATRP) has been considered to be an efficient strategy for constructing functional macromolecules owing to its simple operation and versatile monomers, and thus it is of great significance to develop ideal catalysts with higher activity and perfect reusability. We constructed a peroxidase mimic through the grafting of heme onto metal-organic frameworks UiO-66-NH2 (ZrMOF), namely Heme-ZrMOF. After the systematic characterization of structure, the composite Heme-ZrMOF was demonstrated to possess high peroxidase activity using 2,2'-azinobis-(3-ethylbenzthiazoline-6-sulphonate) and 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine as substrates. The enzyme mimic was then used as catalysts in the ATRP reactions of different monomers, in which favorable monomer conversion $(44.6\mathrm{-}98.0\%)$ and product molecular weight (8,600-25,600 g/mol) could be obtained. Compared to free heme, Heme-ZrMOF could efficiently achieve the easy separation of heme from the catalytic system and facilitate the ATRP reaction in an aqueous environment to avoid the utilization of organic solvents. In conclusion, the enzyme mimic Heme-ZrMOF could be potentially used as an effective catalyst for preparing well-defined polymers with biomedical applications.
Keywords: Peroxidase mimic; Metal-organic frameworks; Heme; Atom transfer radical polymerization; Polymer synthesis
## 1. Introduction
The rapid growth of polymeric materials with predictable molecular weight, narrow distribution, well-defined architecture and delicate monomer sequence has required the construction of versatile catalysts and synthetic methods. In the past two decades, atom transfer radical polymerization (ATRP) has developed rapidly and become an efficient strategy for synthesizing various functional macromolecules owing to its characteristics of mild reaction conditions, simple operation and versatile monomers [1-4]. However, there were many factors which limited the application of ATRP reactions in the synthesis of biomedical polymers, such as metal residue and unsatisfactory reusability [5,6]. Recently, several catalytic systems with low toxicity and excellent biocompatibility have been successfully developed for executing ATRP reactions, such as iron-based catalysts [7-15]. Nevertheless, the lower activity and selectivity was still a key issue to be solved for facilitating the application scope of these catalysts. Thus, it is urgent to design iron-based systems with higher activity, no metal residue and perfect reusability.
Besides iron-based catalysts, certain proteins or enzymes containing metal atoms at the active sites have been proved to possess excellent ATRP catalytic activity, such as horseradish peroxidase (HRP), laccase, catalase and hemoglobin [16-20]. These proteins or enzymes can be named as ATRPase [20], which have dramatically enlarged the scope of monomers due to these catalysts’ multiple structure and favorable pH tolerance. Iron porphyrin has been identified to be the active center of these proteins for executing
ATRP [21], which means that they are intrinsically the immobilized form of iron-based catalyst on the protein backbone. As enzymatic ATRP still faces the drawbacks of sensitivity to reaction conditions and steric hindrance at the active site [19], constructing enzyme mimic harboring iron porphyrin with superior properties to native proteins will be beneficial for achieving the broader application of these bioinspired catalysts. Our previous reports have shown that Deuterohemin- $\upbeta$ -Ala-His-Thr-Val-Glu-Lys (DhHP-6) could be used as an environment benign ATRPase for constructing block copolymers [22] and its immobilized form on metal-organic frameworks (MOF) constructed via biomimetic mineralization could further facilitate the easy separation of catalyst from the system and the elimination of iron residue in the synthesized polymer [23]. However, these catalysts still have several problems to be solved, such as the complicated steps for the synthesis of DhHP-6 and the limited stability of its immobilized form during the catalysis. As heme is the active center of DhHP-6, the immobilization of heme on the solid supports through the covalent bonding will be a more effective technique to prepare ideal ATRPase which might show superior stability to those obtained in a non-covalent manner. In our previous research, heme has been successfully conjugated onto MOF to design an enzyme mimic which showed a favorable catalytic activity in the removal of phenol residue in wastewater [24].
Herein, heme was conjugated with the amino group of 2-amino-1,4-benzene dicarboxylate $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) in UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ (ZrMOF) to construct a peroxidase mimic Heme-ZrMOF, in which 1,1’-carbonyldiimidazole (CDI) was used as an activator (Scheme 1). The peroxidase activity of enzyme mimic was systematically characterized, and then it was employed as catalyst in the ATRP reactions to evaluate its performance.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Heme was kindly provided by Dr. Liping Wang from School of Life Sciences, Jilin University (Changchun, China). 2,2'-Azinobis-(3-ethylbenzthiazoline-6-sulphonate) (ABTS), 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), L-ascorbic acid (L-Asc), $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC and CDI $(97\%)$ were purchased from Energy Chemical and used as received. Ethyl 2-bromo-2-phenylacetate (EBPA, $98\%$ ) was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich and used as received. Poly(ethylene glycol) methyl ether methacrylate $(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{n}}{=}500~\mathrm{g/mol},$ PEGMA500), glycidyl methacrylate (GMA), methacrylic acid (MAA) and 2-(dimethylamino)ethyl methacrylate (DMAEMA) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich and passed through aluminum oxide prior to use in order to remove the inhibitor. All other chemicals were of the highest grade and used without further purification unless otherwise noted.
## 2.2. Construction of peroxidase mimic Heme-ZrMOF
The carrier ZrMOF was first synthesized according to the previous report [25]. Briefly, $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\left(0.60\mathrm{~g},\:2.056\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ and distilled water ${\displaystyle(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ , 10.976 mmol) were mixed in $120~\mathrm{mL}$ dimethyl formamide (DMF) under ultrasonication for $1\ \mathrm{min}$ , and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC (0.62 g, 2.056 mmol) was then added into the system as a linker. The reaction sample was kept in an oven at $120~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then cooled to room temperature after reaction. The precipitate was obtained through the centrifugation at 8,000 rpm for $5\mathrm{min}$ and re-suspended in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, and the suspension was stirred at room temperature for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and subjected to the centrifugation at 8,000 rpm for 5 min. The product was washed with dichloromethane $\mathrm{50~mL}$ , ethanol $(50~\mathrm{mE})$ ) and water (50 mL) to obtain the yellow solid ZrMOF, which was lyophilized and dried under reduced pressure.
To complete the conjugation of heme with ZrMOF, ZrMOF (0.4 g) was dispersed in 7 mL anhydrous dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), to which CDI solution ( $\mathrm{0.5~g}$ in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ DMSO) was added at room temperature. The mixture was stirred at room temperature under nitrogen for $24\mathrm{h},$ , and then 10 mL DMSO solution containing heme $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ and triethyleneamine (TEA, 1 mL) was added dropwise into the mixture. The reaction was conducted under nitrogen at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the reaction mixture was precipitated and washed with excess DMSO, DMF, acetone and water, and finally subjected to lyophilization to obtain Heme-ZrMOF.
## 2.3. Characterization of peroxidase mimic Heme-ZrMOF
The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra were obtained on a Bruker Avance III NMR spectrometer $(400~\mathrm{{MHz}})$ ) with DMSO- ${\bf\cdot D}_{6}$ including trace hydrofluoric acid as solvent, in which chemical shifts (in ppm) were recorded using tetramethylsilane as an internal standard. Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) were measured on a Shimadzu FTIR 8400S spectrometer. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were measured on a PANalytical B.V. Empyrean powder diffractometer, in which data were collected from $5^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}$ to $45^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}$ at a scanning rate of $15~\mathrm{{^{o}m i n}}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were captured on a Hitachi FE-SEM S-4800 instrument  with an acceleration voltage of $3\mathrm{kV},$ , in which the samples were prepared by depositing sample dispersion onto a freshly cleaved silicon wafers. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was performed on a JEM-2100F filed emission electron microscope with an acceleration voltage of $200\ \mathrm{kV}.$ Solid-state ultraviolet-visible spectroscopy (UV-Vis) spectra were measured on a Shimadzu UV-2550 spectrophotometer. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms were determined by a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 system, and Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface was obtained. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out on a TA Q500 thermogravimetric analyzer and data were collected from 100 to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a scanning rate of $10\mathrm{^{o}C/m i n}$ . The diameter of ZrMOF and Heme-ZrMOF was measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS) on a Zetasizer Nano ZS90 instrument, in which the samples were dispersed in alcohol.
## 2.4. Peroxidase activity assay of Heme-ZrMOF
The peroxidase activity of Heme-ZrMOF was measured using TMB and ABTS as substrates in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , respectively. For TMB as substrate, heme $(1.38~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ or Heme-ZrMOF $(50.0~\upmu\upmathrm{g})$ were added into the substrate solution containing TMB
$(0.8\mathrm{-}4.0\ \mathrm{mM})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ${\bf\zeta}20{-}100{\bf\ m}{\bf M})$ at room temperature, and the absorbance at 652 nm within 1 min was monitored using UV-2550 spectrophotometer. In addition, the catalytic kinetics was evaluated through Michaelis-Menten equation. For ABTS as substrate, Heme-ZrMOF $(50.0~\upmu\mathrm{g})$ was added into the substrate solution harboring ABTS $(0.01{-}0.20~\mathrm{mM})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{i0~mM)}$ , and the absorbance at 403 nm within 1 min was measured using UV-2550 spectrophotometer.
## 2.5. ATRP activity assay of Heme-ZrMOF
In a typical experiment, Heme-ZrMOF (60 mg) and monomers $(7.0\ \mathrm{mmol})$ ) were dissolved in $4\mathrm{\mL}$ DMF (for monomers GMA and MMA) or distilled water (for monomers PEGMA500 and DMAEMA) in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ branch-necked flask, followed by purging with nitrogen for 0.5 h. Then $0.10~\mathrm{mL}$ L-Asc solution (0.35 mmol in distilled water) was added into the reaction mixture. The flask was sealed under nitrogen and immersed in an oil bath $(50~\mathrm{^{o}C)}$ , and $6~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ EBPA (0.07 mmol) was added to initiate the reaction. The samples were filtrated to remove the catalyst after $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ , and excess dimethyl ether was added into the filtrate to precipitate the polymer. The produced polymer was obtained through the centrifugation at $8{,}000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , dissolved in dichloromethane, re-precipitated in dimethyl ether three times and dried under vacuum. The reaction samples were taken from the flask at predetermined intervals for $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR and gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis to monitor the ATRP activity. The GPC assay was performed on a Malvern 515 HPLC apparatus with Malvern 410 RI detector and Agilent PLgel MIXED-D columns using tetrahydrofuran or DMF as the eluent at a flow of $1.0\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ at $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , in which polystyrene standards of narrow molecular weight distribution were employed for the calibration.
## 3. Results and discussion
In the present research, ZrMOF was first synthesized from ZrCl4 and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC with four equivalents of water according to the previous report [25], and then heme was conjugated with the amino group of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC in ZrMOF to construct the peroxidase mimic Heme-ZrMOF using CDI as an activator (Scheme 1). The SEM and TEM morphologic characterization indicated that Heme-ZrMOF exhibited a regular spherical shape with relatively smooth surface (Fig. 1a-1c). The average diameter of particles was observed of ca. $200\mathrm{nm}$ from SEM images, while the value was measured to be $489\mathrm{nm}$ through DLS method (Fig. S1). These results were probably caused by the crosslinking between ZrMOF in which heme acted as a cross-linking agent owing to its two carboxyl groups. In addition, physical appearance photos showed that Heme-ZrMOF possessed a dark red color while ZrMOF and Heme $@$ ZrMOF constructed via physical adsorption were yellow and dark yellow, respectively (Fig. 1d). These results demonstrated that Heme-ZrMOF was successfully synthesized through the conjugation of the carboxyl group of heme and the amino group of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC.
To further ascertain that heme was grafted onto ZrMOF crystalline shell, the obtained Heme-ZrMOF was characterized by PXRD, FT-IR, $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR and solid state UV-Vis spectra (Fig. S2-S5). The PXRD pattern of Heme-ZrMOF exhibited peaks matching well with the as-synthesized ZrMOF, suggesting that the structural integrity of ZrMOF framework could be retained in the presence of heme. In the FT-IR spectrum of Heme-ZrMOF, new peaks at $1035~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1087~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were observed which was probably caused by the formation of anhydride between ZrMOF and heme. The unique vibration at $1620~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ could be attributed to the C-N stretching mode in the amide group, which provided a direct evidence for the conjugation of heme with ZrMOF. After the digestion by hydrofluoric acid, Heme-ZrMOF was characterized by $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ , which clearly showed the co-existence of characteristic peaks in $\mathrm{\bfBDC-NH}_{2}$ and new peaks from the conjugation. Moreover, the characteristic peak at $596~\mathrm{nm}$ could be clearly observed in the solid state UV-Vis spectrum of Heme-ZrMOF, which also demonstrated the successful conjugation of heme with ZrMOF. Based on the absorbance at $400~\mathrm{nm}$ , the amount of heme conjugated on ZrMOF was calculated to be ca. $2.76~\mathrm{wt}\%$ . Further, nitrogen sorption isotherms were measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ to assess the porosity of ZrMOF and Heme-ZrMOF (Fig. S6 and S7). The isotherm of Heme-ZrMOF exhibited a Type I isotherm with hysteresis loop at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}{=}0.25$ , and BET surface area of Heme-ZrMOF was measured to be $372.90~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ which was much smaller than the as-synthesized ZrMOF $(543.86~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ ), revealing that the conjugation of heme would decrease the surface area of ZrMOF (Table S1). The TGA curves showed that the first weight loss of Heme-ZrMOF in air happened in the range of $200{-}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(5.61~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ , which could be attributed to the decomposition of heme (Fig. S8). The value was much higher than that determined through UV-Vis spectra $(2.76~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ ), which was mainly caused by the difference between these two methods. In fact, there were still some components in the supernatant after centrifugation which might influence the measurement accuracy of UV-Vis spectra.
The peroxidase activity of enzyme mimic Heme-ZrMOF was first measured using ABTS $\mathrm{(0.01{-}0.20\ m M)}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{i}0\ \mathrm{mM})$ as substrates. In this reaction system, Heme-ZrMOF could catalyze the consumption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce hydroxyl radical which would facilitate the conversion of ABTS to ABTS• +, and thus the formation of ABTS• + could be monitored at $403~\mathrm{nm}$ and used to measure the enzymatic activity. As shown in Fig. 2, an obviously increasing absorbance at $403\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be detected owing to the production of ABTS• , which clearly elucidated that the composite Heme-ZrMOF possessed favorable peroxidase activity. In addition, the catalytic activity exhibited an increasing tendency with the improvement of ABTS concentration. Further, the peroxidase activity of enzyme mimic Heme-ZrMOF was measured using TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as substrates. As shown in Fig. 3, the reaction velocity vs. substrate concentration followed a typical Michaelis-Menten curve in the tested range of substrate concentration. Through Lineweaver-Burk plot, the Michaelis-Menten constant $\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ and the maximum initial velocity $(V_{\mathrm{max}})$ were calculated and summarized in Table 1. For the substrate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , Heme-ZrMOF showed a slightly decreased $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value and increased $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ value compared to heme, indicating the enzyme mimic possessed higher substrate affinity and catalytic efficiency. Heme-ZrMOF also exhibited an enhanced binding ability toward the substrate TMB. Remarkably, compared to heme, it could dramatically increase the $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ value up to ca. 3 times for the substrate TMB. The enhanced substrate affinity and catalytic efficiency could be attributed to these two reasons: (1) Heme-ZrMOF could improve the dispersion of catalyst in the aqueous medium and avoid the formation of heme aggregates; and (2) Heme-ZrMOF could enhance the specific surface area compared to heme, and thus it would be favorable for the substrates’ binding and further catalysis.
Further, the ATRP activity of Heme-ZrMOF was measured using different monomers (PEGMA500, DMAEMA, GMA and MMA). To identify whether the polymerization of GMA was conducted in Heme-ZrMOF-mediated ATRP reaction, the reactions were carried out in water at $50~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ using EBPA and PEG-Br as initiators, respectively. The $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra of the products after purification were shown in Fig. 4. The characteristic peaks of PGMA $\scriptstyle\mathtt{\delta=0.93}$ and $1.90\mathrm{ppm}$ ) could be clearly observed in these two products. The presence of PEG peaks indicated the successful preparation of PEG-co-PGMA. The conversion of GMA could be calculated based on the integral area of two vinyl protons of residual monomer $\scriptstyle\langle\delta=5.71$ and $6.13~\mathrm{ppm})$ and the overlapping signal $(\updelta{=}1.90\ \mathrm{ppm}_{\cdot}$ ). As shown in Table 2, the conversion values of GMA were in the range of $44.6\substack{-58.5\%}$ which were lower than PEGMA500 $(73.0\%)$ and DMAEMA $(98.0\%)$ , when the reactions were conducted with [Monomer]/[Initiator] of 100-400:1. The phenomenon was mainly caused by the reaction media, as the polymerization of $\mathrm{PEGMA_{500}}$ and DMAEMA was performed in DMF which was a suitable medium for achieving the heme-catalyzed
ATRP reaction. Generally, ATRP reactions mediated by iron-based catalysts were suitable in organic solvents, but the conversions were still low [26-28]. In the present research, the conversion could be improved through the construction of Heme-ZrMOF and meanwhile the polymerization of GMA could be achieved in aqueous environment. Through Heme-ZrMOF-mediated ATRP reactions, the polymers could be obtained with high molecular weight $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{n}}$ values of 8,600-25,600 g/mol).
Finally, reaction kinetics of Heme-ZrMOF-mediated ATRP were investigated using GMA as the monomer at [Monomer]/[Initiator] of 100:1, in which the conversion of GMA as a function of reaction time and the evolution of molecular weight and its distribution were determined by $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR and GPC analysis. As free heme could be dissolved in DMF, it showed an obviously stronger reaction rate than Heme-ZrMOF (Fig. 5a). However, Heme-ZrMOF still possessed favorable ATRP catalytic activity and exhibited great potential in reducing the metal residue. At the beginning, the conversion vs. reaction time showed a linear state indicating that the concentration of growing radicals was constant, whereas the conversion would not increase with the elongation of reaction time (after 40 and 60 min for heme and Heme-ZrMOF, respectively). In this system, the reaction was triggered by the addition of small amount of reducing agent L-Asc, which made the conversion of ATRP catalyst from the oxidatively stable precursor to the lower oxidation state $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ . The alkyl halides could react with Heme-ZrMOF Fe(II) to form growing radicals $(\mathrm{P_{n}\bullet})$ and dormant species Fe(III)-Br, and thus the equilibrium between propagating radicals and dormant species was established.
The excess accumulation of Fe(III) caused by the chain termination could be activated by the reducing agent L-Asc. Thus, the termination of polymerization might be caused by the depletion of L-Asc or the quenching of growing radicals. Meanwhile, the product molecular weight showed an independent manner on the monomer conversion (Fig. 5b), which was similar to the enzymatic polymerization using thermophilic esterases as catalysts [29,30]. However, these were not consistent with the previous reports on the proportional increasing of $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{n}}$ value with conversion [19,31]. This phenomenon was probably caused by three reasons: (1) the enzyme mimic possessed a less efficient initiation ability and an unmatched equilibrium between the deactivation reaction and the shift to dormant species; (2) the accessibility of enzyme’s active site depended on the size of polymer chain, and the enzyme mimic showed a lower probability to reach the active site for longer polymeric chains, owing to the large steric hindrance; and (3) the enzyme mimic itself might act as a chain termination agent by the addition of the radical of growing chain to Heme-ZrMOF in its resting Fe(III) state. The detailed mechanism was still underway in our laboratory for getting a deeper insight into the enzyme mimic-catalyzed ATRP reaction and facilitating the construction of biomedical polymers through this green and sustainable route.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, an enzyme mimic Heme-ZrMOF was successfully constructed through the grafting of heme onto ZrMOF, which showed favorable peroxidase activity and
ATRP catalytic ability. Compared to free heme, Heme-ZrMOF could efficiently achieve the easy separation of catalyst from the product, which is beneficial for reducing the residue of metallic catalysts and thus enlarging the application scope in the biomedical field. Meanwhile, the enzyme mimic could realize the polymerization of different monomers in an aqueous medium, which will avoid the utilization of organic solvents and construct a green and sustainable synthetic process. All these results demonstrated that the enzyme mimic Heme-ZrMOF could be employed as an effective catalyst in the preparation of biomedical polymers. The deeper understanding on the reaction kinetics and mechanism of enzyme mimic-mediated ATRP reaction is still underway in our laboratory. Meanwhile, we will focus on the construction of tailor-made polymeric materials using this unique biocatalyst, especially at a large scale.
